|> Exercise
    handle=_isosets_of_xy_v5_

    What are the maximum and minimum values
    of 

    $$
    \theta \ra \cos\theta\sin\theta
    $$

    and for which $\theta$ are they attained?
    (Reason geometrically or algebraically, not by calculus.)

    |> Solution

        _Solution 1._ For a geometric solution we start by
        reminding to ourselves that if we plot all the solutions of

        $$
        xy = 1
        $$

        we find the curve 

        $$
        y = {1\over x}
        $$

        (equivalent!) whose general aspect is...

        |> Image
            src=images/_e5_max_cosx_sinx_one_over_x_graph.svg
            width=470px

        ...like so; a little more generally,
        “isosets” of the two-variable function...

        $$
        (x, y) \ra xy
        $$

        ...have this general aspect...

        |> Image
            src=images/svg_ch5_ex_max_xy_xy_isosets.svg
            style=margin-bottom:0.5em;

        ...where you see the curve “$xy = 1$” among a
        mix of other such curves, of the form $xy = C$;
        what will give us a maybe even better level of
        insight is the following “heatmap” of $(x, y) \ra xy$,
        where the curves above show up as boundaries between
        the regions of different color:
        !! drawn here in the same $[-4, 4] \times [-4, 4]$ window:

        !! These isosets give rise to the boundaries
        !! between colored regions 
        !! when we draw a “heatmap” of $(x, y) \ra xy$,
        !! drawn here in the same 
        !! $[-4, 4] \times [-4, 4]$ 
        !! window:

        |> Image
            src=images/svg_ch5_ex_max_xy_rainbow.svg
            style=margin-bottom:0.5em;

        The geometric insight that is of interest to
        !! us, and that we accept without further proof,
        is that,
        at any given
        distance from the origin, the lines

        $$
        x = y
        $$

        and

        $$
        x = -y
        $$

        !! seem to 
        hold the largest and smallest values, respectively,
        of $xy$:

        |> Image
            src=images/svg_ch5_ex_max_xy_fixed_distance_v2.svg

        In other words,
        $$
        \cos(\theta)\cdot\sin(\theta),
        $$
        as a value of the form “$x$-coordinate times $y$-coordinate”
        of a point confined to the unit circle,
        will reach its maximum when 

        $$
        (\cos \theta, \sin \theta) = \pm(\sqrt{0.5}, \sqrt{0.5})
        $$

        where it attains value

        $$
        !! \sqrt{0.5} \cdot \sqrt{0.5} = (-\sqrt{0.5}) \cdot (-\sqrt{0.5}) = {1\over 2}
        \sqrt{0.5} \cdot \sqrt{0.5} = {1\over 2}
        $$

        and will reach its minimum when

        $$
        (\cos \theta, \sin \theta) = \pm(-\sqrt{0.5}, \sqrt{0.5})
        $$

        where it attains value 

        $$
        !! (-\sqrt{0.5}) \cdot \sqrt{0.5} = \sqrt{0.5} \cdot (-\sqrt{0.5}) = -{1\over 2}
        (-\sqrt{0.5}) \cdot \sqrt{0.5} = -{1\over 2}
        $$

        while, to answer the last part of the question,
        the set of $\theta$'s for which the 
        maximum and minimum are reached will be...

        $$
        \{0.5\eta + 2\eta k: k \in \zz\}
        $$

        ...and...

        $$
        \{1.5\eta + 2\eta k: k \in \zz\}
        $$

        ...respectively, as illustrated...

        |> Image
            src=images/svg_ch5_ex_max_xy_angles_v2.svg

        ...here.

        |> SolutionNote

            !! We can draw annotated graphs of $\cos x\sin x$, in 
            !! radians and in degrees:
            Here are annotated graphs of $x \ra \cos x\sin x$, in 
            radians and in degrees, proudly displaying the newly
            discovered max & min:
            !! For interest, here is the graph $y = \cos(x)\sin(x)$,
            !! as previously given in Exercise >>_four_graphs_, annotated
            !! with the maximum, minimum, and the values $x$ at which they
            !! are attained:

            |> Image
                src=images/svg_ch5_ex_max_xy_graph_v2.svg
                style=margin-bottom:-0.5em

            |> Image
                src=images/svg_ch5_ex_max_xy_graph_degrees.svg
